{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nCanonical bridge operations expose conventional process statuses only: 0 for success or benign no-work/yield/paused states, 1 for operational failure, and 2 for invalid invocation/configuration. Legacy reconcile routes historically emit 3 for another live pass, 4 for the phase gate, and 75 for reschedule. Duplicated numeric whitelists in workflow and library callers had diverged.\n\nMove semantic classification into one typed core disposition. Canonical `rebar bridge preview` / `sync` translate it to 0/1/2. Legacy `rebar reconcile --mode ...` and direct `python -m rebar_reconciler --mode ...` retain historical codes, messages, and defaults for older workflows, systemd/Jenkins units, scripts, and environments. New and old routes share one result and differ only in outer compatibility translation.\n\nPrerequisite 1104 defines paused as benign while retaining legacy gate behavior. Prerequisite 4a44 retains old command/engine flags. Sibling f789 exposes the shared result through public Python and MCP adapters.\n\n## Core disposition and exact mapping\n\nThe reconciler boundary distinguishes converged, paused, live pass in flight, reschedule, legacy phase gate, invalid invocation/configuration, and operational failure. Core owns semantic facts/messages; adapters own only numeric translation and never rerun or reclassify a pass.\n\nCanonical preview/sync map the first five benign dispositions to 0, invalid invocation/configuration to 2, and operational failure to 1. Exact benign canonical stderr is:\n\n- converged: `BRIDGE_STATE: converged`\n- paused: existing `BRIDGE_PAUSED: {...}` single-line JSON marker\n- in flight: `BRIDGE_STATE: in-flight`\n- reschedule: `BRIDGE_STATE: reschedule`\n- historical phase gate: `BRIDGE_STATE: legacy-gated`\n\nLegacy CLI/direct-engine adapters map the same dispositions to existing 0/3/4/75/1/2 results and retain legacy messages/defaults. Library/MCP bridge adapters consume the same typed disposition; legacy `rebar.reconcile` and MCP `reconcile` continuity remains.\n\n## Workflow and compatibility rollout\n\nMigrate `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml` to the canonical bridge invocation and remove its numeric case whitelist. It accepts canonical 0, fails on 1/2, and preserves the exact paused marker plus commit-and-push skip.\n\nSearch deployment units and tracked infrastructure for consumers of 3/4/75, including systemd `RestartForceExitStatus=75`, Jenkins shells, workflow conditions, and probes. Every in-tree consumer either migrates here or is explicitly classified as legacy compatibility. Older checked-out workflow revisions remain functional because the retained reconcile adapter still emits their expected codes.\n\nDocument both contracts: bridge is 0/1/2; reconcile compatibility retains historical translations. Retirement of legacy codes is a separate future deprecation after old entrypoints leave support.\n\n## Verification\n\nUse real subprocess entrypoints over deterministic temporary repositories. Seed every disposition and exercise canonical bridge plus legacy CLI/direct-engine routes. Assert exact status, message, one-pass execution, and identical repository/ref effects. Preview remains non-mutating and sync preserves its existing mutation semantics.\n\nExecute the production workflow block to prove the old numeric whitelist is absent, canonical 0/1/2 is sufficient, paused still skips commit/push, and 1/2 fail. Keep an old-workflow/legacy-command fixture proving 3/4/75 continuity.\n\nRepository census fails on an unclassified primary-surface 3/4/75 consumer while allowing the legacy translation table, compatibility tests, and dual-contract docs. No new contention harness is required because this classifies results after existing lock/CAS seams and introduces no retry, cache, or composed write.\n\nSurface coverage:\n\n- CLI and direct engine: canonical subcommands plus legacy reconcile/`--mode` subprocesses.\n- Python library and MCP: shared disposition boundary here; public facade parity in f789.\n- Automated callers: current and legacy workflow fixtures plus deployment/probe inventory.\n- Dual direction: preview and sync share disposition mapping without changing state semantics.\n\nProof at the merged epic ref includes `test_reconcile_main.py`, `test_bridge_cli_heldout.py`, `test_lock_steal.py`, and `test_reconcile_workflow_lint.py`. `docs/exit-codes.md` and `docs/release-notes.md` record canonical 0/1/2, retained 3/4/75, and rolling-deployment continuity. Gerrit change 1595 merged after LLM-Review +1 and Verified +1.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] Canonical preview/sync emit only 0/1/2; every benign disposition exits 0 with the exact canonical marker and no canonical adapter emits 3/4/75.\n- [x] Legacy reconcile CLI and direct-engine `--mode` retain historical 3/4/75 translations, messages, and defaults for old workflows/environments.\n- [x] Canonical and legacy adapters consume one typed disposition, run the pass once, and preserve identical repository/ref effects.\n- [x] Python and MCP bridge/legacy consumers use the shared result boundary without a divergent whitelist.\n- [x] The production workflow uses canonical 0/1/2, has no old case whitelist, and preserves paused commit-skip behavior.\n- [x] Deployment/workflow/script/probe 3/4/75 consumers are migrated or explicitly classified compatibility.\n- [x] Exit-code and release docs publish both contracts and the rolling-migration guarantee.\n- [x] Focused subprocess, workflow, library, and MCP continuity tests pass at the merged Verified +1 ref."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786437095317549001,"uuid":"ab6ba09c-d1e7-45a7-9885-96165bd784ef"}